<h1>Response to Reviewer Comments & Methodological Justifications</h1>
<p>This document provides professional explanations and justifications addressing the methodological queries raised during the review process. All corresponding datasets, scripts, and replication materials have been integrated into the central repository.</p>

<h2>Issue 1: Comparative Evaluation of GMM against Random Forest and Baselines</h2>
<p><span class="bold">Reviewer Query:</span> Request for a comparative scenario map demonstrating the proposed method's performance against baselines like Random Forest, Otsu, and NDWI across diverse landscapes.</p>
<p><span class="bold">Explanation:</span> A 5-panel comparative map for a diverse morphological region has been generated and included in the repository. Crucially, the ST-GMM demonstrates distinct advantages over machine learning approaches like Random Forest in this decadal study. Random Forest requires extensive, high-quality, and temporally matched training data for every timestamp to generalize well across an 11-year period. It is prone to overfitting localized training data and struggles with temporal transferability in highly dynamic monsoon environments. Conversely, ST-GMM is an unsupervised, spatiotemporally adaptive statistical model. It dynamically extracts the water-land threshold directly from the instantaneous backscatter distribution of each specific scene. By not relying on external training data, ST-GMM is significantly more robust, scalable, and temporally consistent for long-term (2015&ndash;2025) surface water mapping.</p>

<h2>Issue 2: Per-Class Accuracy Across Different Hydroperiods</h2>
<p><span class="bold">Reviewer Query:</span> Request for accuracy metrics broken down by specific water classes (Permanent, Semi-permanent, and Ephemeral) rather than relying solely on overall accuracy.</p>
<p><span class="bold">Explanation:</span> We have conducted a granular accuracy assessment. Using the JRC Global Surface Water occurrence data, we stratified our 4,310 ground truth points into Permanent (&ge; 80% occurrence), Semi-permanent (40&ndash;79%), and Ephemeral (1&ndash;39%) classes. We computed the User's Accuracy (UA) and Producer's Accuracy (PA) for each specific class. The evaluation confirms that the ST-GMM framework maintains robust detection capabilities across all dynamic hydroperiods, successfully detecting ephemeral and seasonal inundations without being biased solely toward permanent water bodies. The comprehensive accuracy table has been provided in the repository.</p>

<h2>Issue 3: Verification of Validation Points (Latitude and Longitude Coordinates)</h2>
<p><span class="bold">Reviewer Query:</span> How can reviewers verify the exact spatial locations and coordinate details of the field validation points used in this study?</p>
<p><span class="bold">Explanation:</span> To ensure full data transparency, the complete set of 4,310 validation points with exact coordinate details is provided in the repository under <code>data/GEE_Upload_Ready_LatLon.csv</code>. Reviewers and researchers can verify these points using the following methods:
<ul>
<li><span class="bold">Direct Inspection:</span> Open the <code>GEE_Upload_Ready_LatLon.csv</code> file, which lists the exact <code>latitude</code>, <code>longitude</code>, <code>Month</code>, <code>Year</code>, and <code>Field_Truth</code> (0 for land, 1 for water) for each point.</li>
<li><span class="bold">GIS Import:</span> The CSV file can be directly imported into standard GIS software (such as QGIS or ArcGIS Pro) as a Delimited Text Layer using WGS 84 (EPSG:4326) coordinate system to visualize the spatial distribution across Bangladesh.</li>
<li><span class="bold">Google Earth Engine (GEE) Upload:</span> The CSV contains columns formatted for direct ingestion as a <code>FeatureCollection</code> in the GEE Code Editor to overlay onto satellite imagery.</li>
</ul>
</p>

<h2>Issue 4: Adequacy of the Two-Component GMM (AIC/BIC Justification)</h2>
<p><span class="bold">Reviewer Query:</span> Is a two-component GMM adequate? The reviewers requested goodness-of-fit diagnostics (AIC/BIC) comparing the two-component GMM against three- or four-component alternatives.</p>
<p><span class="bold">Explanation:</span> We performed a rigorous Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) and Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC) analysis across varied geomorphological districts. While adding 3 or 4 components can sometimes marginally reduce statistical error by fitting minor terrain noise or soil moisture variations, it mathematically leads to severe overfitting. The 2-component GMM perfectly encapsulates the fundamental physical bimodality of SAR backscatter in riverine and flood-prone landscapes: low backscatter (Water) and high backscatter (Land/Vegetation). Therefore, the 2-component model provides the most physically meaningful, generalizable decision boundary without overfitting to local noise.</p>

<h2>Issue 5: Influence of Optical NDWI Initialization on SAR-Based Claims</h2>
<p><span class="bold">Reviewer Query:</span> Does initializing the GMM with optical NDWI compromise the claim that the accuracy and methodology are strictly "SAR-based", especially given optical cloud-cover limitations?</p>
<p><span class="bold">Explanation:</span> We clarify that the final classification is 100% SAR-based. The optical NDWI is utilized strictly as an initial seed (prior probability) to accelerate the Expectation-Maximization (EM) algorithm's convergence rate. The final decision boundary (the threshold) is determined autonomously by the geometric intersection of the two Gaussian curves, which are fitted entirely on the Sentinel-1 SAR VV backscatter distribution. The mathematical outcome of the GMM is completely independent of the optical NDWI's inherent accuracy or its cloud-cover limitations, ensuring the method remains robust in monsoon conditions.</p>

<h2>Issue 6: Reproducibility and Code Availability</h2>
<p><span class="bold">Reviewer Query:</span> Request to consolidate all accuracy datasets, points, methodology, and locations into one unified, clear format.</p>
<p><span class="bold">Explanation:</span> The entire codebase has been restructured into a clean, professional, and automated repository. All raw datasets, shapefile coordinates, and validation points are hosted centrally. An end-to-end Replication Notebook has been provided that automatically clones the repository and processes the raw data, allowing reviewers and researchers to reproduce all figures, tables, and accuracy metrics instantly without manual setup.</p>

</body>
